<div class="cf-footnotes">
    <h5>Footnotes</h5>
        <ol>
                    <li id="cf-fn-1">See Fratianni and von Hagen (2001).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-1" aria-label="Return from footnote 1.">Return to 1</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-2">See Mester (2014), Bernanke (2006), and Plosser (2011).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-2" aria-label="Return from footnote 2.">Return to 2</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-3">See Const&acirc;ncio (2015).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-3" aria-label="Return from footnote 3.">Return to 3</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-4">See Chart 5 in Fischer (2015).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-4" aria-label="Return from footnote 4.">Return to 4</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-5">This model is an AR(1) with time-varying parameters and stochastic volatility, related to a specification considered in Cogley, Primiceri, and Sargent (2010).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-5" aria-label="Return from footnote 5.">Return to 5</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-6">See Clark and Garciga (2016).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-6" aria-label="Return from footnote 6.">Return to 6</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-7">For example, Faust and Wright (2013) provide evidence of the value of longer-run survey expectations of professional forecasters for forecasting inflation at shorter horizons.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-7" aria-label="Return from footnote 7.">Return to 7</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-8">Larkin (2016) shows the effect of oil price increases on inflation expectations in the U.S., Euro area, Israel, and U.K. pre- and post-crisis.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-8" aria-label="Return from footnote 8.">Return to 8</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-9">Note that the San Francisco Fed series shown is daily data while the Cleveland Fed series is monthly data and so that series appears smoother. The Cleveland Fed produces estimates of inflation expectations and the inflation risk premium that combine data on nominal yields, inflation swaps, and surveys of inflation expectations to separately identify these factors.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-9" aria-label="Return from footnote 9.">Return to 9</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-10">As Faust and Wright (2013) discuss, for five-year, five-year forward inflation compensation to be a rational expectation of inflation in the long term, it would have to be a martingale, i.e., its expected value tomorrow would be equal to its value today. An implication is that the volatility of k-period changes in forward inflation compensation must be k times the volatility of one-period changes. They test and reject this hypothesis.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-10" aria-label="Return from footnote 10.">Return to 10</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-11">See Const&acirc;ncio (2015).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-11" aria-label="Return from footnote 11.">Return to 11</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-12">See, for example, Orphanides (2015), Orphanides and Van Norden (2005), and Orphanides (2002).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-12" aria-label="Return from footnote 12.">Return to 12</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-13">For an accessible description of the New Keynesian Phillips curve, see Sill (2011).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-13" aria-label="Return from footnote 13.">Return to 13</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-14">See the discussion in Sill (2011).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-14" aria-label="Return from footnote 14.">Return to 14</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-15">See Minutes of the FOMC Meeting of January 26-27, 2016.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-15" aria-label="Return from footnote 15.">Return to 15</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-16">See The Macroeconomic Model Data Base (MMB) web page at www.macromodelbase.com for more information on the database; for a discussion of the approach, see Wieland, Cwik, M&uuml;ller, Schmidt, and Wolters (2012).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-16" aria-label="Return from footnote 16.">Return to 16</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-17">See Yellen (2015) and Trehan and Lynch (2013).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-17" aria-label="Return from footnote 17.">Return to 17</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-18">See Coibion and Gorodnichenko (2015).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-18" aria-label="Return from footnote 18.">Return to 18</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-19">See Bils and Klenow (2004), and Nakamura and Steinsson (2008).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-19" aria-label="Return from footnote 19.">Return to 19</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-20">See Gilchrist, Schoenle, Sim, and Zakrajsek (2015).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-20" aria-label="Return from footnote 20.">Return to 20</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-21">Aruoba and Schorfheide (2015).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-21" aria-label="Return from footnote 21.">Return to 21</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-22">See Aruoba and Schorfheide (2015) and Aruoba, Bocola, and Schorfheide (2013).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-22" aria-label="Return from footnote 22.">Return to 22</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-23">See, for example, Blanchard, Dell&rsquo;Ariccia, and Mauro (2010), and Rosengren (2015).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-23" aria-label="Return from footnote 23.">Return to 23</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-24">George W. Evans and Seppo Honkapohja have done seminal work in learning and macroeconomics. See their review article, Evans and Honkapohja (2009). See also Orphanides and Williams (2008).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-24" aria-label="Return from footnote 24.">Return to 24</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-25">An interesting paper (Assenza, Heemeijer, Hommes, and Massaro, 2013) studies individuals&rsquo; formation of expectations in a standard New Keynesian model within a laboratory setting with human subjects. The authors find that the economy converges to an equilibrium but with oscillations and that, consistent with the theoretical literature, a monetary policy rule that reacts more than one for one to changes in inflation stabilizes inflation.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-25" aria-label="Return from footnote 25.">Return to 25</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-26">Bernanke (2007) discusses some of these ideas.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-26" aria-label="Return from footnote 26.">Return to 26</a>
                    </li>
        </ol>
</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="component notes col-12">
        <div class="component-content">
<h5 class="field-title">References</h5><ul>
    <li>Aruoba, Borağan, Luigi Bocola, and Frank Schorfheide, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.nber.org/papers/w19693" target="_blank" class="offsite">Assessing DSGE Model Nonlinearities</a>,&rdquo; National Bureau of Economic Research Working Paper 19693, 2013.</li>
    <li>Aruoba, Borağan, and Frank Schorfheide, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.kansascityfed.org/~/media/files/publicat/sympos/2015/econsymposium-aruoba-schorfheide-paper.pdf?la=en&amp;hash=554A9CBB37E15440300BCA087153B7C1" target="_blank" class="offsite">Inflation During and After the Zero Lower Bound</a>,&rdquo; Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City Economic Policy Symposium, Jackson Hole, WY, August 2015.</li>
    <li>Assenza, Tiziana, Peter Heemeijer, Cars Hommes, and Domenico Massaro, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=2200424" target="_blank" class="offsite">Individual Expectations and Aggregate Macro Behavior</a>,&rdquo; Tinbergen Institute Discussion Paper 13-016/II, January 5, 2013.</li>
    <li>Bank of England,&nbsp;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.bankofengland.co.uk/publications/Documents/inflationreport/2016/feb.pdf" target="_blank" class="offsite"><em>Inflation Report</em></a>, February 2016.</li>
    <li>Bernanke, Ben S., &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/bernanke20060224a.htm" target="_blank" class="offsite">The Benefits of Price Stability</a>,&rdquo; Center for Economic Policy Studies, Princeton University, Princeton, New Jersey, February 24, 2006.</li>
    <li>Bernanke, Ben S., &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/bernanke20070710a.htm" target="_blank" class="offsite">Inflation Expectations and Inflation Forecasting</a>,&rdquo; remarks at the Monetary Economics Workshop of the National Bureau of Economic Research Summer Institute, Cambridge, MA, July 10, 2007.</li>
    <li>Bils, Mark, and Peter J. Klenow, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://klenow.com/StickyPrices.pdf" target="_blank" class="offsite">Some Evidence on the Importance of Sticky Prices</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Journal of Political Economy</em>&nbsp;112, 2004, pp. 947-985.</li>
    <li>Blanchard, Olivier, Giovanni Dell&rsquo;Ariccia, and Paolo Mauro, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1538-4616.2010.00334.x/epdf" target="_blank" class="offsite">Rethinking Macroeconomic Policy</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Journal of Money, Credit, and Banking</em>&nbsp;42, 2010, pp. 119-215.</li>
    <li>Clark, Todd, and Christian Garciga, &ldquo;<a href="https://clevelandfed-qa-cd.ase2.p.azure.frb.pvt/Collections/Speeches/2016/~/media/281B52EBA2FA48438DB2F95A15593D7E.ashx">Recent Inflation Trends</a>,&rdquo; Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland Economic Trends, January 14, 2016.</li>
    <li>Cogley, Timothy, Giorgio E. Primiceri, and Thomas J. Sargent, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/mac.2.1.43" target="_blank" class="offsite">Inflation-Gap Persistence in the US</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>American Economic Journal: Macroeconomics</em>&nbsp;2, 2010, pp. 43-69.</li>
    <li>Coibion, Olivier, and Yuriy Gorodnichenko, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/mac.20130306" target="_blank" class="offsite">Is the Phillips Curve Alive and Well after All? Inflation Expectations and the Missing Disinflation</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>American Economic Journal: Macroeconomics</em>&nbsp;7, January 2015, pp. 197-232.</li>
    <li>Const&acirc;ncio, V&iacute;tor, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.kansascityfed.org/~/media/files/publicat/sympos/2015/econsymposium-constancio-paper.pdf?la=en&amp;hash=93D5B53BA7863EC1D9F9C7C9E65E10DF" target="_blank" class="offsite">Understanding Inflation Dynamics and Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo; remarks at the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City Economic Policy Symposium, Jackson Hole, WY, August 29, 2015.</li>
    <li>European Central Bank,&nbsp;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.ecb.europa.eu/pub/pdf/other/ecbstaffprojections201603.en.pdf?b04a09832bebde6edaa7798807a7ea28" target="_blank" class="offsite"><em>ECB Staff Macroeconomic Projections for the Euro Area</em></a>, March 2016.</li>
    <li>Evans, George W., and Seppo Honkapohja, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.annualreviews.org/doi/abs/10.1146/annurev.economics.050708.142927" target="_blank" class="offsite">Learning and Macroeconomics</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>The Annual Review of Economics</em>&nbsp;1, 2009, pp. 421-449.</li>
    <li>Faust, Jon, and Jonathan H. Wright, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780444536839000013" target="_blank" class="offsite">Forecasting Inflation,&rdquo; in&nbsp;<em>Handbook of Economic Forecasting</em></a>, Graham Elliott and Allan Timmermann, eds., Amsterdam: Elsevier Press, vol. 2A, 2013, pp. 2-56.</li>
    <li>Fischer, Stanley, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/fischer20150829a.pdf" target="_blank" class="offsite">U.S. Inflation Developments</a>,&rdquo; remarks at the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City Economic Policy Symposium, Jackson Hole, WY, August 29, 2015.</li>
    <li>Fratianni, Michele, and J&uuml;rgen von Hagen, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.wiwi.uni-bonn.de/konstanz/Konstanz_Seminar_at_Thirty.pdf" target="_blank" class="offsite">The Konstanz Seminar on Monetary Theory and Policy at Thirty</a>,&rdquo; May 2001.</li>
    <li>Gilchrist, Simon, Raphael Schoenle, Jae W. Sim, and Egon Zakrajsek, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://dx.doi.org/10.17016/FEDS.2015.012" target="_blank" class="offsite">Inflation Dynamics During the Financial Crisis</a>,&rdquo; Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Finance and Economics Discussion Series 2015-012, March 3, 2015.</li>
    <li>Larkin, John, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.centralbank.ie/publications/Documents/Quarterly%20Bulletin%20No.%202%202016.pdf" target="_blank" class="offsite">Box A: Oil Prices and Inflation Expectations: An Investigation Into the Contribution of Global Demand and Oil Supply Shocks to Euro Area Inflation Expectations</a>,&rdquo; in Central Bank of Ireland,&nbsp;<em>Quarterly Bulletin</em>&nbsp;02, April 2016, pp. 47-48.</li>
    <li>Mester, Loretta J., &ldquo;<a href="/collections/speeches/2014/sp-20140530-inflation-and-monetary-policy-six-research-questions">Inflation and Monetary Policy: Six Research Questions</a>,&rdquo; speech before the 2014 Inflation Conference hosted by the Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, May 29, 2014.</li>
    <li><a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20160127.htm" target="_blank" class="offsite">Minutes of the FOMC Meeting of January 26-27, 2016</a>.</li>
    <li>Nakamura, Emi, and J&oacute;n Steinsson, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.columbia.edu/~en2198/papers/fivefacts.pdf" target="_blank" class="offsite">Five Facts about Prices: A Reevaluation of Menu Cost Models</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Quarterly Journal of Economics</em>, November 2008, pp. 1415-1464.</li>
    <li>Orphanides, Athanasios, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://fraser.stlouisfed.org/docs/publications/aer/aer_orphanides_monpol_200205.pdf" target="_blank" class="offsite">Monetary Policy Rules and the Great Inflation</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>American Economic Review</em>&nbsp;92, 2002, pp. 115&ndash;120.</li>
    <li>Orphanides, Athanasios, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.kansascityfed.org/~/media/files/publicat/sympos/2015/econsymposium-orphanides-handout.pdf?la=en&amp;hash=C1316062EFD3CA00657D661F5A6056F2" target="_blank" class="offsite">Inflation Dynamics: Lessons From Past Debates for Current Policy</a>,&rdquo; remarks at the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City Economic Policy Symposium, Jackson Hole, WY, August 29, 2015.</li>
    <li>Orphanides, Athanasios, and Simon Van Norden, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://econpapers.repec.org/article/mcbjmoncb/v_3a37_3ay_3a2005_3ai_3a3_3ap_3a583-601.htm" target="_blank" class="offsite">The Reliability of Inflation Forecasts Based on Output Gap Estimates in Real Time</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Journal of Money, Credit, and Banking</em>, 37, 2005, pp. 583-601.</li>
    <li>Orphanides, Athanasios, and John C. Williams, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304393208001098" target="_blank" class="offsite">Learning, Expectations Formation, and the Pitfalls of Optimal Control Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Journal of Monetary Economics</em>&nbsp;55, 2008, S80-S96.</li>
    <li>Plosser, Charles I., &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.philadelphiafed.org/publications/speeches/plosser/2011/04-14-11_20th-annual-minsky-conference.cfm" target="_blank" class="offsite">Strengthening Our Monetary Policy Framework</a>,&rdquo; 20th Annual Hyman P. Minsky Conference, New York, NY, April 14, 2011.</li>
    <li>Rosengren, Eric S., &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.bostonfed.org/news/speeches/rosengren/2015/041615/041615text.pdf" target="_blank" class="offsite">Changing Economic Relationships: Implications for Monetary Policy and Simple Monetary Policy Rules</a>,&rdquo; Chatham House, London, England, April 16, 2015.</li>
